<%#
  THE EDIT MODE'S TOP BAR (2026-09-09; spec §2). Save is the ONE write: a real form to the panels
  door whose hidden `layout` field the controller fills with the draft, then posts AS DATA so a
  refusal keeps the draft on screen (layout_editor_controller.js#submit). Cancel is a link (nothing
  to undo server-side) and, like Done and every other way off the page, asks first while the draft
  differs from the saved layout. Reset is the existing DELETE, after a confirm, because it removes
  the override rather than editing the draft; its form tells the editor once that confirm is
  answered, so the visit it ends in does not ask a second question. The noscript line is the whole
  of the no-JavaScript story (spec decision 8).

  THE EDIT AREA'S THREE TABS AGREE (2026-11-02, the UX audit's C6): each Save names what it saves
  (Save changes, Save publishing, Save layout) and is followed by the same Cancel, a plain
  `linkbtn` back to the project.

  THE EDITOR IS ALWAYS A WHOLE DOCUMENT (2026-11-02): `turbo-visit-control: reload` makes a Turbo
  link that arrives here load the page afresh instead of rendering it into the document it came
  from. Without it, browser Back from a dirty editor was a Turbo RESTORATION visit — which fires
  neither `turbo:before-visit` nor `beforeunload` — and the draft went with no question. As its own
  document, Back crosses documents and `beforeunload` asks (page_editor_test.rb). The cost is one
  extra request on the way in. Here, not in projects/show, because this bar is rendered in edit mode
  and nowhere else; the head yields `content_for(:head)` (layouts/_head).
%>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <meta name="turbo-visit-control" content="reload">
<% end %>
<div class="layout-bar">
  <div class="layout-bar-text">
    <b>Editing page layout</b>
    <span class="muted">Drag panels by their handle. Half-width panels pair up on wide screens. Nothing is saved until you click Save.</span>
    <noscript><span class="layout-bar-noscript">The page editor needs JavaScript.</span></noscript>
  </div>
  <div class="layout-bar-actions">
    <%#
      ADD BLOCK (2026-09-09, content blocks; spec §2). The new block lands FIRST in the grid with
      its editor open, which is why this sits in the BAR rather than as an insertion point between
      panels: one control, one destination, and dragging is how it gets anywhere else.

      DISABLED AT THE CAP, SAID OUT LOUD. Maker::LayoutDocument refuses a document over
      Maker::ProjectBlock::CAP and Save refuses again — but a button that silently does nothing is
      the worse failure, so the door's own number is rendered here rather than spelled a second time.

      `details.menu` IS THE APP'S OVERFLOW MENU and its `.menu-list`/`.menu-item` classes are
      components.css's; `data-controller="menu"` is what closes it on an outside click.
    %>
    <% at_cap = @blocks.size >= Maker::ProjectBlock::CAP %>
    <details class="menu layout-add" data-controller="menu" data-layout-editor-target="addMenu">
      <summary class="btn small">Add block ▾</summary>
      <div class="menu-list">
        <% { "text" => "Text", "link" => "Link", "image" => "Image" }.each do |kind, name| %>
          <button type="button" class="menu-item" data-action="layout-editor#addBlock"
                  data-kind="<%= kind %>" <%= "disabled" if at_cap %>><%= name %></button>
        <% end %>
        <% if at_cap %>
          <p class="muted layout-add-cap">This page has <%= Maker::ProjectBlock::CAP %> blocks, the most it can hold.</p>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </details>
    <%# ADD TAB (2026-09-09, tabs; spec §2): a new, empty tab lands at the BOTTOM — a new separator at
        the top would swallow every panel into one tab. Disabled at
        Maker::ProjectKind::TAB_CAP with the door's own number, like Add block — AND THE REASON IS
        TEXT ON THE PAGE, NOT A `title` (the UX audit's C10): a tooltip on a disabled button is
        unreachable by keyboard and by touch, the two places a dead control most needs explaining. %>
    <% tabs_at_cap = @panel_layout.tabs.size >= Maker::ProjectKind::TAB_CAP %>
    <button type="button" class="btn small" data-action="layout-editor#addTab" <%= "disabled" if tabs_at_cap %>
            <% unless tabs_at_cap %>title="A new tab at the bottom; move panels under it, or move it up."<% end %>>Add tab</button>
    <% if tabs_at_cap %>
      <span class="muted layout-add-cap layout-tab-cap">This page has <%= Maker::ProjectKind::TAB_CAP %> tabs, the most it can hold.</span>
    <% end %>
    <%# "AS TEXT", NOT "AS YAML" (the UX audit's §H): the format's name is the app's business. What a
        maker needs to know is that the arrangement can be copied out and pasted back. %>
    <button type="button" class="btn small" data-action="layout-editor#toggleDrawer" aria-expanded="false">Edit as text</button>
    <%= button_to "Reset to #{Maker::ProjectKind.label(@project.kind)} defaults", project_panels_path(@project),
                  method: :delete, class: "linkbtn small",
                  form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Reset the page to its type's defaults? Order, hidden panels, views, widths, titles, intros and tabs all go back.",
                                  action: "turbo:submit-start->layout-editor#discard turbo:submit-end->layout-editor#resumeGuard" } } %>
    <%= form_with url: project_panels_path(@project), method: :patch, class: "layout-save",
                  data: { layout_editor_target: "form", action: "submit->layout-editor#save" } do %>
      <%= hidden_field_tag :layout, "", data: { layout_editor_target: "draft" }, id: "layout-draft" %>
      <%= submit_tag "Save layout", class: "btn small primary" %>
    <% end %>
    <%= link_to "Cancel", project_path(@project), class: "linkbtn edit-cancel" %>
  </div>
</div>
